[[:db/retract 17592186327814 :question/answer-fields 17592186327815] [:db/retract 17592186327814 :question/answer-fields 17592186327817] {:db/id "q-128339/mathematical-correction-v1/field-1/answer-0" :answer/id #uuid "73cf6ac4-f0f0-5512-a57b-e7378900b295" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128339/mathematical-correction-v1/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "85758217-04a2-5aa3-8ddd-e0c46cc59e54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["q-128339/mathematical-correction-v1/field-1/answer-0"] :answer-field/correct "q-128339/mathematical-correction-v1/field-1/answer-0" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128339/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-0" :answer/id #uuid "f1fea7ea-193d-5611-8661-4485ed158ebf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128339/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "af6489b1-05a1-55da-9195-4e8d26b2515a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/choices ["q-128339/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-0"] :answer-field/correct "q-128339/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2/answer-0" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186327814 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}$. Find the coefficients $A$ and $B$ in its partial-fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1},\\qquad x\\ne3,-\\frac12.$$\n\n$A=$ {{field-1}}, $B=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Factor the denominator:\n\n$$2x^2-5x-3=(x-3)(2x+1).$$\n\nMultiplying the partial-fraction identity by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3)=(2A+B)x+(A-3B).$$\n\nMatching coefficients yields $2A+B=0$ and $A-3B=7$. Thus $B=-2A$, so $7A=7$ and\n\n$$A=1,\\qquad B=-2.$$\n\nSubstitution verifies the identity because $(2x+1)-2(x-3)=7$.\n\nIf this decomposition is used to evaluate the improper integral from $4$ to infinity, keep a common finite upper bound $R$ and take the limit only after combining the terms:\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty f(x)\\,dx=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\left(\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{x-3}-2\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{2x+1}\\right)=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}=\\ln\\frac92.$$\n\nThe two separate improper integrals each diverge, so they cannot be subtracted as independently evaluated improper integrals." :question/answer-fields ["q-128339/mathematical-correction-v1/field-1" "q-128339/mathematical-correction-v1/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
